---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'methods', label: 'Automated, hybrid, and manual' },
   { id: 'numbers', label: 'Criteria per level' },
   { id: 'strategy', label: 'How to test a hybrid or manual criterion' },
   { id: 'lookup', label: 'Looking up a criterion' },
   { id: 'claims', label: 'Reporting results' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Test methods"
   summary="Every success criterion in the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG), the standard most accessibility requirements come from, has a test method: automated, hybrid, or manual. It says whether a1 axe decides the criterion, whether a person has to, and what to run in between. a11ied derives it from the W3C ACT rule mapping and the axe-core rule tags."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="ledger" id="methods">
      <h2>Automated, hybrid, and manual</h2>
      <p>
         A WCAG success criterion is one numbered requirement, such as 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value, at
         level A, AA, or AAA. For each one, a11ied records which of these applies. The JSON output
         of <code>a1 wcag</code> returns it as the <code>method</code> field of <code>testMethod</code>.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>automated</code>
            <span>
               One or more axe rules decide the criterion. <code>a1 axe --criterion 1.4.3</code>
               runs them, and a pass means the criterion is met.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>hybrid</code>
            <span>
               Automated checks test part of the criterion and a person tests the rest. WCAG 2.2 has ten
               criteria with this method, including 2.4.3 Focus Order and 4.1.3 Status Messages.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>manual</code>
            <span>
               No axe rule or ACT rule maps to the criterion. <code>a1 wcag</code> prints the
               requirement, the techniques, and the failures, and a person tests the page.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>unknown</code>
            <span>
               The mapping data did not classify the criterion. No criterion in WCAG 2.1 or 2.2 has
               this value.
            </span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="numbers">
      <h2>Criteria per level</h2>
      <p>WCAG 2.2 has 86 success criteria. <code>a1 wcag criteria --summary</code> counts them by level and test method:</p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag criteria --summary</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>WCAG 2.2 test methods  updated 2026-09-05
  Level      Criteria   Automated  Hybrid     Manual     Unknown
  A          31         15         3          13         0
  AA         24         7          4          13         0
  AAA        31         5          2          24         0
  All        86         27         9          50         0

  28 criteria have an axe rule, 21 have an ACT rule, 19 have both.</code></pre>
      <p>
         Each criterion's record in the JSON output lists where its test method came from under
         <code>sourceAttribution</code>. The five values are <code>act-mapping</code>,
         <code>axe-rule-tags</code>, <code>axe-act-id-join</code>,
         <code>strategy-heuristic</code>, and <code>manual-fallback</code>. See
         <a href="/reference/wcag-data">WCAG data</a> for the upstream sources.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="strategy">
      <h2>How to test a hybrid or manual criterion</h2>
      <p>
         Each criterion also has a <code>strategy</code> that lists the test procedure and says
         whether the page has to be open in a browser or screen reader.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag 4.1.3 --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"testMethod": &#123;
  "method": "hybrid",
  "axeRuleIds": [],
  "actRuleIds": [],
  "sourceAttribution": ["strategy-heuristic"]
&#125;,
"strategy": &#123;
  "preferredEvidenceMode": "hybrid",
  "procedureIds": ["status_message_probe"],
  "requiresRealTarget": true,
  "notes": [
    "Status messages need announcement evidence from assistive technology without forced focus changes."
  ]
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>requiresRealTarget: true</code> means reading the HTML is not enough: the test needs
         the page open in a browser or a screen reader session. For 4.1.3 Status Messages, that is
         a session in which you trigger the update, then run <code>a1 sr transcript</code> to see
         whether the screen reader announced it. The text output of <code>a1 wcag 4.1.3</code> says
         the same thing in its <code>Testing it</code> block, with the command to run.
      </p>
      <p>
         A criterion with <code>"procedureIds": ["axe_scan"]</code> needs only
         <code>a1 axe</code>. A criterion with <code>["manual_review"]</code> needs a person.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="lookup">
      <h2>Looking up a criterion</h2>
      <p>
         Every lookup reads data bundled with the package. None of them opens a browser or makes a
         network request.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag                                  # interactive finder on a terminal
a1 wcag 4.1.3                            # one criterion: requirement, how to test it, how to fix it
a1 wcag G18                              # one technique and the criteria that list it
a1 wcag criteria --level AA --json
a1 wcag criteria --summary               # criteria per level and test method
a1 search "focus order" --kind criterion --limit 3 --json
a1 wcag rule color-contrast              # axe rule to criteria and fix guidance</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>a1 wcag &lt;criterion&gt;</code> takes an ID such as <code>4.1.3</code>, a slug such as
         <code>status-messages</code>, or a technique ID such as <code>G18</code>. It prints the
         requirement text, the sufficient and advisory techniques, the failures, the test method,
         the mapped axe and ACT rules, and the command to run next. Use <code>search</code> when you
         know the topic but not the number. It ranks matches across titles, summaries, requirement
         text, techniques, failures, and tags. <code>a1 wcag rule &lt;axe-rule-id&gt;</code> goes
         the other way: from an axe violation to the criteria it fails and the techniques that fix
         it.
      </p>
      <p>
         Every <code>wcag</code> command takes <code>--wcag</code>. WCAG 2.1 and 2.2 are both in the
         package, and 2.2 is the default.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="claims">
      <h2>Reporting results</h2>
      <p>
         A passing <code>a1 axe --level AA</code> run decides some of the level AA criteria and
         says nothing about the rest. It does not mean the page meets level AA.
      </p>
      <p>
         Report the test method next to each result: the criteria <code>a1 axe</code> decided, the
         criteria a screen reader transcript supports, and the criteria still waiting on a person.
         <code>a1 audit</code> prints that split for one page under <code>Check by hand</code>.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
